<h2>Starting with the Touchpoints</h2>

<p>The existing materials did not share a consistent visual language. Business documents, production collateral, and digital surfaces needed to feel like parts of the same organization.</p>

<p>I approached the work as a system rather than a single logo: identify every repeated touchpoint, set practical rules, then review those rules against print constraints and screen layouts.</p>

<h2>The Research Phase</h2>

<p>Before opening Figma, I studied how garment manufacturers present technical capability without losing clarity. The strongest references used restrained typography, limited color, and consistent document hierarchy.</p>

<p>That research became a set of constraints: the identity had to work on ordinary office printers, remain legible on production documents, and scale into the website.</p>

<h2>Making the system repeatable</h2>
<p>The completed <a href="/work/brand-identity">brand identity case study</a> records the deliverables. The useful lesson was how to make each new document predictable.</p>
<p>I defined a heading hierarchy, a restrained palette, repeatable spacing and separate export settings for print and screen. A company profile, a letterhead and a production card have different jobs, but their typography and layout rules should still belong to the same system.</p>

<h2>Design Principles I Discovered</h2>

<p>Through this project, I learned a few things that no tutorial taught me:</p>

<p><strong>Consistency matters more than creativity.</strong> A simple logo used consistently across every touchpoint builds more trust than a clever design used inconsistently.</p>

<p><strong>Design for the context.</strong> A trim card that goes inside a garment box needs to survive shipping. A letterhead needs to look good printed on a basic office printer. Designing for real-world use changes your decisions.</p>

<p><strong>Start with typography.</strong> Choose the right fonts first and the rest of the identity builds around them naturally. I picked a professional serif for headings and a clean sans-serif for body text. Everything else followed.</p>

<h2>The Result</h2>

<p>The final system gives the company a repeatable visual language across its profile, stationery, production collateral, and website. The value is consistency: new materials can be produced without reopening basic decisions every time.</p>

<p>Design completion and distribution are different checkpoints. Keeping that distinction clear makes the handover easier: finished artwork, export specifications and usage guidance can be reviewed independently of a print order.</p>

